-- gate: reject
-- defect: an ALIASED import of a module that does not exist
-- rust: reject [E1001] unknown Sky module, reported at the import
--
-- Found 2026-08-11 by the generated reject matrix (`xtask corpus --reject`,
-- v2 §3.1 family R), which crosses defect classes against import shapes. This
-- program used to be ACCEPTED: `sky check` printed "Names resolved", "Types OK",
-- emitted Go, `go build` succeeded, and the binary panicked at run time with
--
--     Sky panic: TypeMismatch — rt.AsInt: expected numeric value, got <nil>
--
-- Both "if it compiles, it works" and "no runtime panic from well-typed Sky"
-- were false for a one-character typo in an import.
--
-- ROOT CAUSE. `SourceDb::classify_import` (hir/src/db.rs) resolves parsed dep >
-- kernel pseudo > **Foreign**, and that last arm is a TOTAL fallback: every
-- unrecognised path becomes a Go-FFI package, whose references resolve leniently
-- to `nil` by design (the documented class-(b) FFI contract). A Sky-namespaced
-- path can never be a Go package, so for `Std.*` / `Sky.*` that leniency was a
-- soundness hole.
--
-- WHY IT SURVIVED. The one shape anybody had tried WAS covered: a direct CALL
-- through the unknown module (`Nope.compute 1`) already produced lower.rs's
-- "unknown Sky module" error. Three other shapes reached the same fallback with
-- no check at all — a bare value reference (this file), a type reference
-- (`Nope.Thing`), and an import that is never used. Also note the un-aliased
-- spelling `import Std.NoSuchModule` + `Std.NoSuchModule.answer` was correctly
-- rejected [E1001] the whole time; only the ALIAS path was open, which is the
-- same import-alias resolution seam as #164.
--
-- FIX. `hir::resolve::process_import` checks the classification at the IMPORT,
-- so all four shapes are rejected by one rule and the diagnostic points at the
-- line the user has to change. `hir::is_reserved_sky_namespace` is the single
-- definition of the predicate; `lower.rs`'s call-site message now reads it
-- instead of keeping the private copy that let the two drift.
module Main exposing (main)

import Sky.Core.Prelude exposing (..)
import Std.Log exposing (println)
import Std.NoSuchModule as Nope

main =
    println (String.fromInt Nope.answer)
